WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:04.856 --> 00:14.246 align:middle
Chair Yellen's Press Conference
September 20, 2017 CHAIR YELLEN.

00:14.246 --> 00:15.826 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:15.826 --> 00:19.336 align:middle
At our meeting that concluded
earlier today, my colleagues

00:19.336 --> 00:22.406 align:middle
and I on the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

00:22.746 --> 00:24.686 align:middle
to maintain the target range

00:24.686 --> 00:29.016 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate at 1 to 1¼ percent.

00:29.816 --> 00:33.046 align:middle
This accommodative policy
should support some further

00:33.046 --> 00:36.006 align:middle
strengthening in the
job market and a return

00:36.006 --> 00:37.716 align:middle
to 2 percent inflation,

00:38.096 --> 00:40.646 align:middle
consistent with our
statutory objectives.

00:41.576 --> 00:45.936 align:middle
We also decided that in October
we will begin the balance sheet

00:45.936 --> 00:49.606 align:middle
normalization program
that we outlined in June.

00:50.486 --> 00:54.826 align:middle
This program will reduce our
securities holdings in a gradual

00:54.826 --> 00:56.146 align:middle
and predictable manner.

00:56.826 --> 00:59.646 align:middle
I'll have more to say about
these decisions shortly,

00:59.986 --> 01:03.016 align:middle
but first I'll review
recent economic developments

01:03.016 --> 01:03.816 align:middle
and the outlook.

01:04.686 --> 01:08.766 align:middle
As we expected, and smoothing
through some variation

01:08.766 --> 01:10.206 align:middle
from quarter to quarter,

01:10.646 --> 01:13.496 align:middle
economic activity has
been rising moderately

01:13.496 --> 01:14.696 align:middle
so far this year.

01:15.516 --> 01:17.596 align:middle
Household spending
has been supported

01:17.596 --> 01:19.886 align:middle
by ongoing strength
in the job market.

01:20.616 --> 01:22.486 align:middle
Business investment
has picked up,

01:22.906 --> 01:25.496 align:middle
and exports have shown
greater strength this year,

01:26.016 --> 01:29.806 align:middle
in part reflecting improved
economic conditions abroad.

01:30.726 --> 01:34.896 align:middle
Overall, we expect that the
economy will continue to expand

01:34.896 --> 01:37.826 align:middle
at a moderate pace over
the next few years.

01:38.766 --> 01:42.606 align:middle
In the third quarter, however,
economic growth will be held

01:42.606 --> 01:45.666 align:middle
down by the severe
disruptions caused

01:45.706 --> 01:48.896 align:middle
by Hurricanes Harvey,
Irma, and Maria.

01:49.846 --> 01:52.996 align:middle
As activity resumes and
rebuilding gets under way,

01:53.266 --> 01:55.426 align:middle
growth likely will bounce back.

01:56.136 --> 01:59.876 align:middle
Based on past experience,
these effects are unlikely

01:59.876 --> 02:01.916 align:middle
to materially alter the course

02:01.916 --> 02:06.276 align:middle
of the national economy beyond
the next couple of quarters.

02:06.916 --> 02:09.246 align:middle
Of course, for the
families and communities

02:09.246 --> 02:14.366 align:middle
that have been devastated by the
storms, recovery will take time,

02:14.656 --> 02:16.816 align:middle
and on behalf of
the Federal Reserve,

02:17.186 --> 02:20.116 align:middle
let me express our
sympathy for all those

02:20.116 --> 02:21.526 align:middle
who have suffered losses.

02:22.406 --> 02:26.786 align:middle
In the labor market, job gains
averaged 185,000 per month

02:27.186 --> 02:31.266 align:middle
over the three months ending in
August-a solid rate of growth

02:31.266 --> 02:35.496 align:middle
that remained well above
estimates of the pace necessary

02:35.496 --> 02:38.056 align:middle
to absorb new entrants
to the labor force.

02:38.836 --> 02:41.066 align:middle
We know from some
timely indicators,

02:41.066 --> 02:44.706 align:middle
such as initial claims for
unemployment insurance,

02:45.126 --> 02:48.386 align:middle
that the hurricanes severely
disrupted the labor market

02:48.386 --> 02:49.966 align:middle
in the affected areas,

02:50.426 --> 02:54.166 align:middle
and payroll employment may
be substantially affected

02:54.166 --> 02:55.176 align:middle
in September.

02:55.776 --> 02:59.386 align:middle
However, such effects should
unwind relatively quickly.

03:00.206 --> 03:02.916 align:middle
Meanwhile, the unemployment
rate has stayed low

03:02.916 --> 03:07.096 align:middle
in recent months and, at
4.4 percent in August,

03:07.426 --> 03:12.466 align:middle
was modestly below the median
of FOMC participants' estimates

03:12.466 --> 03:14.376 align:middle
of its longer-run normal level.

03:15.486 --> 03:18.466 align:middle
Participation in the labor
force has changed little,

03:18.936 --> 03:21.946 align:middle
both recently and over
the past four years.

03:22.646 --> 03:24.756 align:middle
Given the underlying
downward trend

03:24.756 --> 03:28.366 align:middle
in participation stemming
largely from the aging

03:28.366 --> 03:30.006 align:middle
of the U.S. population,

03:30.516 --> 03:34.386 align:middle
a relatively steady
participation rate is a further

03:34.386 --> 03:37.256 align:middle
sign of improving conditions
in the labor market.

03:38.716 --> 03:41.176 align:middle
We expect that the job
market will strengthen

03:41.176 --> 03:42.196 align:middle
somewhat further.

03:43.106 --> 03:45.636 align:middle
Turning to inflation,
the 12-month change

03:45.636 --> 03:50.896 align:middle
in the price index for personal
consumption expenditures was 1.4

03:50.896 --> 03:55.186 align:middle
percent in July, down noticeably
from earlier in the year.

03:55.946 --> 03:59.206 align:middle
Core inflation- which
excludes the volatile food

03:59.206 --> 04:02.446 align:middle
and energy categories-has
also moved lower.

04:02.726 --> 04:06.526 align:middle
For quite some time, inflation
has been running below the

04:06.526 --> 04:09.466 align:middle
Committee's 2 percent
longer-run objective.

04:10.196 --> 04:12.706 align:middle
However, we believe
this year's shortfall

04:12.706 --> 04:16.336 align:middle
in inflation primarily
reflects developments

04:16.626 --> 04:20.556 align:middle
that are largely unrelated to
broader economic conditions.

04:20.926 --> 04:25.236 align:middle
For example, one-off
reductions earlier this year

04:25.666 --> 04:27.966 align:middle
in certain categories of prices,

04:28.266 --> 04:32.406 align:middle
such as wireless telephone
services, are currently holding

04:32.406 --> 04:36.356 align:middle
down inflation, but these
effects should be transitory.

04:37.186 --> 04:40.226 align:middle
Such developments
are not uncommon and,

04:40.226 --> 04:43.356 align:middle
as long as inflation
expectations remain reasonably

04:43.356 --> 04:46.046 align:middle
well anchored, are
not of great concern

04:46.046 --> 04:50.166 align:middle
from a policy perspective
because their effects fade away.

04:50.916 --> 04:54.266 align:middle
Similarly, the recent
hurricane-related increases

04:54.266 --> 04:57.946 align:middle
in gasoline prices will
likely boost inflation,

04:58.216 --> 04:59.636 align:middle
but only temporarily.

05:00.426 --> 05:03.346 align:middle
More broadly, with
employment near assessments

05:03.346 --> 05:07.636 align:middle
of its maximum sustainable level
and the labor market continuing

05:07.636 --> 05:11.506 align:middle
to strengthen, the Committee
continues to expect inflation

05:11.506 --> 05:16.116 align:middle
to move up and stabilize around
2 percent over the next couple

05:16.116 --> 05:19.376 align:middle
of years, in line with
our longer-run objective.

05:20.136 --> 05:22.496 align:middle
Nonetheless, our understanding

05:22.496 --> 05:26.886 align:middle
of the forces driving inflation
is imperfect, and in light

05:26.886 --> 05:30.236 align:middle
of the unexpected lower
inflation readings this year,

05:30.696 --> 05:34.316 align:middle
the Committee is monitoring
inflation developments closely.

05:35.256 --> 05:37.426 align:middle
As always, the Committee
is prepared

05:37.426 --> 05:42.246 align:middle
to adjust monetary policy as
needed to achieve its inflation

05:42.246 --> 05:45.296 align:middle
and employment objectives
over the medium term.

05:46.506 --> 05:48.776 align:middle
Let me turn to the
economic projections

05:48.776 --> 05:51.706 align:middle
that Committee participants
submitted for this meeting,

05:52.066 --> 05:54.136 align:middle
which now extend through 2020.

05:55.566 --> 05:58.616 align:middle
As always, participants
conditioned their projections

05:58.926 --> 06:03.486 align:middle
on their own individual views
of appropriate monetary policy,

06:04.026 --> 06:07.606 align:middle
which, in turn, depend on
each participant's assessments

06:08.006 --> 06:10.576 align:middle
of the many factors
that shape the outlook.

06:11.386 --> 06:13.536 align:middle
The median projection for growth

06:13.536 --> 06:17.836 align:middle
of inflation-adjusted gross
domestic product, or real GDP,

06:17.836 --> 06:20.826 align:middle
is 2.4 percent this year

06:21.216 --> 06:24.706 align:middle
and about 2 percent
in 2018 and 2019.

06:25.466 --> 06:28.976 align:middle
By 2020, the median
growth projection moderates

06:28.976 --> 06:33.756 align:middle
to 1.8 percent, in line with
its estimated longer-run rate.

06:34.566 --> 06:38.996 align:middle
The median projection for
the unemployment rate stands

06:38.996 --> 06:43.656 align:middle
at 4.3 percent in the
fourth quarter of this year

06:43.906 --> 06:46.096 align:middle
and runs a little
above 4 percent

06:46.096 --> 06:47.696 align:middle
over the next three years,

06:48.166 --> 06:50.446 align:middle
modestly below the
median estimate

06:50.446 --> 06:52.766 align:middle
of its longer-run normal rate.

06:53.456 --> 06:59.046 align:middle
Finally, the median inflation
projection is 1.6 percent this

06:59.046 --> 07:05.616 align:middle
year, 1.9 percent next year,
and 2 percent in 2019 and 2020.

07:06.556 --> 07:09.006 align:middle
Compared with the
projections made in June,

07:09.506 --> 07:12.746 align:middle
real GDP growth is a
touch stronger this year,

07:13.016 --> 07:19.026 align:middle
and inflation-particularly core
inflation-is slightly softer

07:19.026 --> 07:20.216 align:middle
this year and next.

07:20.706 --> 07:24.146 align:middle
Otherwise, the projections
are little changed from June.

07:24.986 --> 07:29.016 align:middle
Returning to monetary policy,
although the Committee decided

07:29.016 --> 07:31.376 align:middle
at this meeting to
maintain its target

07:31.376 --> 07:35.056 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate, we continue to expect

07:35.146 --> 07:36.516 align:middle
that the ongoing strength

07:36.516 --> 07:40.776 align:middle
of the economy will warrant
gradual increases in that rate

07:41.266 --> 07:45.576 align:middle
to sustain a healthy labor
market and stabilize inflation

07:46.516 --> 07:49.746 align:middle
around our 2 percent
longer-run objective.

07:50.806 --> 07:53.276 align:middle
That expectation is
based on our view

07:53.276 --> 07:56.846 align:middle
that the federal funds rate
remains somewhat below its

07:56.846 --> 08:02.686 align:middle
neutral level-that is, the level

08:02.686 --> 08:06.416 align:middle
that is neither expansionary
nor contractionary

08:06.846 --> 08:09.906 align:middle
and keeps the economy
operating on an even keel.

08:10.816 --> 08:13.066 align:middle
Because the neutral
rate currently appears

08:13.066 --> 08:15.766 align:middle
to be quite low by
historical standards,

08:16.696 --> 08:21.226 align:middle
the federal funds rate would not
have to rise much further to get

08:21.226 --> 08:23.136 align:middle
to a neutral policy stance.

08:23.866 --> 08:26.426 align:middle
But because we also
expect the neutral level

08:26.426 --> 08:29.406 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
to rise somewhat over time,

08:29.986 --> 08:33.846 align:middle
additional gradual rate hikes
are likely to be appropriate

08:33.846 --> 08:38.346 align:middle
over the next few years to
sustain the economic expansion.

08:39.116 --> 08:42.536 align:middle
Even so, the Committee
continues to anticipate

08:42.536 --> 08:44.756 align:middle
that the longer-run
neutral level

08:44.756 --> 08:48.786 align:middle
of the federal funds rate is
likely to remain below levels

08:48.786 --> 08:51.136 align:middle
that prevailed in
previous decades.

08:52.716 --> 08:56.526 align:middle
This view is consistent with
participants' projections

08:56.526 --> 08:58.576 align:middle
of appropriate monetary policy.

08:59.456 --> 09:03.226 align:middle
The median projection for
the federal funds rate is 1.4

09:03.226 --> 09:07.656 align:middle
percent at the end of this
year, 2.1 percent at the end

09:07.656 --> 09:12.396 align:middle
of next year, 2.7 percent
at the end of 2019,

09:12.766 --> 09:15.176 align:middle
and 2.9 percent in 2020.

09:16.036 --> 09:19.796 align:middle
Compared with the projections
made in June, the median path

09:19.796 --> 09:23.186 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
is essentially unchanged,

09:23.526 --> 09:25.216 align:middle
although the median estimate

09:25.216 --> 09:29.736 align:middle
of the longer-run normal value
edged down to 2.8 percent.

09:30.886 --> 09:34.206 align:middle
As always, the economic
outlook is highly uncertain,

09:34.626 --> 09:37.326 align:middle
and participants will
adjust their assessments

09:37.326 --> 09:41.806 align:middle
of the appropriate path for the
federal funds rate in response

09:41.846 --> 09:45.286 align:middle
to changes to their
economic outlooks and views

09:45.286 --> 09:47.066 align:middle
of the risks to their outlooks.

09:47.676 --> 09:50.446 align:middle
Policy is not on
a preset course.

09:51.386 --> 09:54.096 align:middle
As I noted, the Committee
announced today

09:54.096 --> 09:58.006 align:middle
that it will begin its balance
sheet normalization program

09:58.006 --> 09:58.836 align:middle
in October.

09:59.646 --> 10:03.866 align:middle
This program, which was
described in the June addendum

10:04.146 --> 10:07.606 align:middle
to our Policy Normalization
Principles and Plans,

10:08.126 --> 10:11.786 align:middle
will gradually decrease our
reinvestments of proceeds

10:11.786 --> 10:14.506 align:middle
from maturing Treasury
securities

10:15.046 --> 10:18.096 align:middle
and principal payments
from agency securities.

10:18.706 --> 10:22.446 align:middle
As a result, our balance
sheet will decline gradually

10:22.756 --> 10:23.826 align:middle
and predictably.

10:24.546 --> 10:26.586 align:middle
For October through December,

10:26.876 --> 10:30.566 align:middle
the decline in our securities
holdings will be capped

10:30.946 --> 10:34.236 align:middle
at $6 billion per
month for Treasuries

10:34.506 --> 10:37.116 align:middle
and $4 billion per
month for agencies.

10:37.836 --> 10:40.746 align:middle
These caps will gradually
rise over the course

10:40.746 --> 10:43.546 align:middle
of the following
year to maximums

10:43.736 --> 10:46.416 align:middle
of $30 billion per
month for Treasuries

10:46.736 --> 10:49.826 align:middle
and $20 billion per month
for agency securities

10:50.346 --> 10:53.296 align:middle
and will remain in place
through the process

10:53.296 --> 10:55.726 align:middle
of normalizing the size
of our balance sheet.

10:57.006 --> 10:59.336 align:middle
By limiting the volume
of securities

10:59.696 --> 11:02.266 align:middle
that private investors
will have to absorb

11:02.666 --> 11:06.096 align:middle
as we reduce our holdings,
the caps should guard

11:06.096 --> 11:09.266 align:middle
against outsized moves
in interest rates

11:09.266 --> 11:11.676 align:middle
and other potential
market strains.

11:12.106 --> 11:14.836 align:middle
Finally, as we have
noted previously,

11:15.186 --> 11:16.766 align:middle
changing the target range

11:16.806 --> 11:20.106 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate is our primary means

11:20.106 --> 11:22.846 align:middle
of adjusting the stance
of monetary policy.

11:23.546 --> 11:27.656 align:middle
Our balance sheet is not
intended to be an active tool

11:27.656 --> 11:30.646 align:middle
for monetary policy
in normal times.

11:31.356 --> 11:34.726 align:middle
We therefore do not plan
on making adjustments

11:34.786 --> 11:37.686 align:middle
to our balance sheet
normalization program.

11:38.316 --> 11:40.916 align:middle
But, of course, as
we stated in June,

11:41.236 --> 11:45.106 align:middle
the Committee would be
prepared to resume reinvestments

11:45.106 --> 11:49.656 align:middle
if a material deterioration
in the economic outlook were

11:49.656 --> 11:53.646 align:middle
to warrant a sizable reduction
in the federal funds rate.

11:54.396 --> 11:55.006 align:middle
Thank you.

11:55.316 --> 11:57.436 align:middle
I'd be happy to take
your questions.

11:58.796 --> 12:02.366 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER CONDON.

12:04.056 --> 12:05.996 align:middle
Chris Condon, Bloomberg News.

12:08.466 --> 12:11.246 align:middle
Chair Yellen, there's been
extraordinary progress during

12:11.246 --> 12:16.806 align:middle
your term as Chair in lowering
several measures of unemployment

12:16.806 --> 12:19.756 align:middle
and underemployment, and
all while inflation has

12:19.756 --> 12:20.616 align:middle
remained subdued.

12:21.226 --> 12:24.236 align:middle
But some people are even
asking, why stop there?

12:24.406 --> 12:27.576 align:middle
Bill Spriggs, the chief
economist at the AFL-CIO,

12:27.576 --> 12:31.546 align:middle
who I think you know well,
criticized the Fed last week

12:31.546 --> 12:36.236 align:middle
for seeking to maintain
unemployment above 4 percent,

12:36.236 --> 12:40.046 align:middle
which he notes necessarily means
keeping the unemployment rate

12:40.046 --> 12:43.166 align:middle
among black Americans
above 8 percent.

12:43.396 --> 12:46.146 align:middle
He described this as
a deliberate policy

12:46.146 --> 12:50.546 align:middle
to sacrifice many hundreds of
thousands of potential workers

12:50.616 --> 12:53.956 align:middle
and their families out of
fear of future inflation,

12:53.956 --> 12:56.376 align:middle
when in fact the Fed's
preferred measure

12:56.696 --> 13:01.816 align:middle
of inflation has not exceeded 3
percent in more than 25 years.

13:02.476 --> 13:05.706 align:middle
So I'm wondering how you would
respond to his frustration

13:05.706 --> 13:08.816 align:middle
over the Fed's desire to
continue raising rates

13:09.336 --> 13:12.016 align:middle
when core inflation really
shows no sign of heading

13:12.336 --> 13:16.196 align:middle
above the Fed's symmetric
goal for inflation.

13:16.196 --> 13:16.666 align:middle
Thank you.

13:16.726 --> 13:16.986 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

13:17.366 --> 13:18.586 align:middle
So let me first say

13:18.586 --> 13:22.356 align:middle
that employment is a very
important part of our mandate.

13:22.816 --> 13:25.236 align:middle
We're charged by
Congress with trying

13:25.236 --> 13:28.066 align:middle
to pursue maximum employment,

13:28.386 --> 13:30.786 align:middle
and we have taken
that very seriously.

13:30.786 --> 13:35.806 align:middle
I'm very pleased and heartened
by the improvement we have seen

13:36.196 --> 13:39.466 align:middle
in the labor market,
and at 4.4 percent,

13:39.786 --> 13:45.256 align:middle
the unemployment rate has really
fallen to quite a low level.

13:46.166 --> 13:49.496 align:middle
As that's happened,
the unemployment rates

13:49.496 --> 13:53.546 align:middle
for less advantaged
groups in the labor market,

13:53.546 --> 13:57.046 align:middle
particularly African
Americans and Hispanics,

13:57.076 --> 14:01.636 align:middle
has fallen more dramatically
than that for the nation

14:01.636 --> 14:06.256 align:middle
as a whole, reversing
the outsized increases

14:06.256 --> 14:10.226 align:middle
that those groups experienced
when the financial crisis

14:10.226 --> 14:11.736 align:middle
and Great Recession hit.

14:11.736 --> 14:15.086 align:middle
And these are really very
positive developments.

14:15.086 --> 14:20.216 align:middle
So we certainly seek
a strong labor market,

14:20.536 --> 14:24.686 align:middle
but we have a dual
mandate, which is inflation

14:24.906 --> 14:29.856 align:middle
and unemployment, and we
also have to be mindful

14:29.856 --> 14:35.146 align:middle
of our obligation to achieve a
2 percent inflation objective

14:35.446 --> 14:37.076 align:middle
over the medium term.

14:37.856 --> 14:41.696 align:middle
Now, I recognize
and it's important

14:41.696 --> 14:45.626 align:middle
that inflation has been running
under our 2 percent objective

14:45.626 --> 14:50.786 align:middle
for a number of years, and
that is a concern, particularly

14:50.786 --> 14:55.356 align:middle
if it were to translate into
lower inflation expectations.

14:55.446 --> 14:59.816 align:middle
For a number of years there
were very understandable reasons

14:59.816 --> 15:05.336 align:middle
for that shortfall, and they
included quite a lot of slack

15:05.336 --> 15:07.946 align:middle
in the labor market-which,
my judgment would be,

15:07.946 --> 15:12.576 align:middle
has largely disappeared-very
large reductions

15:12.576 --> 15:17.806 align:middle
in energy prices, and a large
appreciation of the dollar

15:18.106 --> 15:23.296 align:middle
that lowered import prices
starting in mid-2014.

15:23.566 --> 15:28.006 align:middle
This year, the shortfall of
inflation from 2 percent,

15:28.356 --> 15:33.036 align:middle
when none of those factors is
operative, is more of a mystery,

15:33.486 --> 15:38.606 align:middle
and I will not say that the
Committee clearly understands

15:38.726 --> 15:40.416 align:middle
what the causes are of that.

15:40.416 --> 15:44.426 align:middle
Now, we do, in our
regular projection,

15:44.426 --> 15:49.786 align:middle
show fan charts indicating the
typical size of forecast errors.

15:49.786 --> 15:53.496 align:middle
All of the variables-GDP,
the unemployment rate,

15:53.496 --> 15:56.736 align:middle
and inflation-are
forecast by ourselves

15:56.736 --> 15:59.366 align:middle
and private forecasters
with errors,

15:59.366 --> 16:02.846 align:middle
and so there is variation

16:02.846 --> 16:07.566 align:middle
in these economic
variables from year to year.

16:07.886 --> 16:10.986 align:middle
I would say our judgment,
as I said in the statement,

16:10.986 --> 16:14.806 align:middle
is that the shortfall
is not largely related

16:14.876 --> 16:18.006 align:middle
to cyclical considerations.

16:19.316 --> 16:21.726 align:middle
You can see from the projections

16:21.726 --> 16:28.336 align:middle
that the Committee participants
submitted that we anticipate

16:28.336 --> 16:32.116 align:middle
that core and headline
inflation will move up close

16:32.116 --> 16:35.586 align:middle
to our 2 percent
objective next year-namely,

16:35.806 --> 16:41.416 align:middle
that the shortfall this year
is due to transitory factors

16:41.416 --> 16:43.806 align:middle
that are likely to disappear

16:43.806 --> 16:45.686 align:middle
over the course of
the coming year.

16:46.006 --> 16:49.106 align:middle
But I want to emphasize
that we do have a commitment

16:49.396 --> 16:52.176 align:middle
to raising inflation
to 2 percent.

16:52.496 --> 16:57.026 align:middle
And as we watch incoming
data, the assessments

16:57.026 --> 17:01.666 align:middle
that you see participants
write down about the path

17:01.666 --> 17:04.966 align:middle
of the federal funds rate,
they are not set in stone.

17:05.196 --> 17:07.256 align:middle
They are not definite plans.

17:07.336 --> 17:11.376 align:middle
We will look at incoming
data on inflation

17:11.696 --> 17:15.596 align:middle
and on other economic variables,
including the labor market,

17:15.596 --> 17:19.656 align:middle
in deciding what we should
actually do going forward.

17:19.656 --> 17:23.476 align:middle
And if it proves contrary
to our expectations

17:23.806 --> 17:27.556 align:middle
that the shortfall is
persistent, it will be necessary

17:27.556 --> 17:30.806 align:middle
to adjust monetary
policy to address that.

17:31.106 --> 17:33.716 align:middle
But I want to point out
that while there are risks

17:33.976 --> 17:39.246 align:middle
that inflation could continue
below 2 percent, which we need

17:39.246 --> 17:41.826 align:middle
to take account of
in monetary policy,

17:42.256 --> 17:45.406 align:middle
monetary policy also
operates with a lag.

17:45.786 --> 17:50.296 align:middle
And experience suggests that
tightness in the labor market,

17:50.476 --> 17:53.936 align:middle
gradually and with a lag,
tends to push up wage

17:53.936 --> 17:58.626 align:middle
and price inflation, and that's
also a risk, that we want

17:58.626 --> 18:03.846 align:middle
to be careful not to allow the
economy to overheat in a way

18:03.846 --> 18:08.876 align:middle
that would force us later
on, somewhere down the road,

18:08.916 --> 18:12.406 align:middle
to have to tighten
monetary policy rapidly,

18:12.406 --> 18:14.126 align:middle
which could cause a recession

18:14.366 --> 18:18.006 align:middle
and threaten the very desirable
labor market conditions

18:18.006 --> 18:18.796 align:middle
that we have now.

18:19.336 --> 18:20.686 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

18:23.696 --> 18:25.166 align:middle
Thanks very much.

18:25.166 --> 18:27.566 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

18:27.756 --> 18:32.486 align:middle
The Fed's staff spoke recently
about elevated asset prices

18:32.736 --> 18:36.336 align:middle
in the markets in the most
recent-in the meeting before

18:36.336 --> 18:38.046 align:middle
this one held today.

18:38.076 --> 18:42.456 align:middle
How are buoyant market
conditions affecting the debate

18:42.456 --> 18:46.096 align:middle
at the moment about how
quickly to rein in stimulus?

18:46.496 --> 18:49.096 align:middle
And have they, in your own
mind, helped to counter some

18:49.096 --> 18:52.336 align:middle
of the concerns you've had
about the inflation shortfalls

18:52.336 --> 18:53.136 align:middle
that we've been seeing?

18:53.746 --> 18:55.986 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR YELLEN.

18:56.336 --> 19:02.416 align:middle
So at every meeting we try to
assess the economic outlook

19:02.416 --> 19:07.296 align:middle
and take account of information
that's been accumulated

19:07.296 --> 19:10.776 align:middle
about the real economy
and also developments

19:10.776 --> 19:14.126 align:middle
in financial markets and
put all of that together

19:14.336 --> 19:16.486 align:middle
in assessing the
course of the economy.

19:16.526 --> 19:20.556 align:middle
So developments affecting
asset prices

19:20.556 --> 19:25.566 align:middle
and longer-term interest
rates, the exchange rate-all

19:25.566 --> 19:29.726 align:middle
of those aspects of
financial conditions factor

19:29.726 --> 19:30.906 align:middle
into our thinking.

19:31.506 --> 19:37.766 align:middle
But it's not easy to get a
clear read on the implications

19:37.766 --> 19:40.766 align:middle
of asset prices for
the overall outlook.

19:40.766 --> 19:46.846 align:middle
Sometimes movements-upward
movements in asset prices can,

19:46.846 --> 19:52.886 align:middle
for example, reflect a change in
market participants'-reduction

19:52.886 --> 19:55.776 align:middle
in market participants'
estimates

19:55.776 --> 19:59.236 align:middle
of the longer-run level
of interest rates.

19:59.236 --> 20:04.876 align:middle
So there has been-there have
been downward revisions both

20:04.976 --> 20:09.896 align:middle
to the Committee's and to
market participants' estimates

20:09.956 --> 20:13.366 align:middle
of the longer-run normal
level of interest rates,

20:13.556 --> 20:18.606 align:middle
which in turn reflects in
some sense a view that,

20:18.606 --> 20:23.796 align:middle
going out many years, aggregate
demand globally is likely

20:23.866 --> 20:29.126 align:middle
to be weakened by continuing
low productivity growth

20:29.126 --> 20:30.866 align:middle
and aging populations.

20:31.056 --> 20:33.866 align:middle
And, of course, we don't
know if that view is correct,

20:33.986 --> 20:37.796 align:middle
but that's a factor
that could be reflected-

20:37.966 --> 20:42.936 align:middle
could be one reason why asset
prices have moved as they have.

20:42.936 --> 20:47.746 align:middle
So, you know, why are
asset prices moving?

20:47.746 --> 20:51.016 align:middle
That's important in
determining the impact

20:51.226 --> 20:56.136 align:middle
on the overall outlook, but,
certainly, we are taking account

20:56.136 --> 20:58.376 align:middle
of movements in asset prices

20:58.546 --> 21:01.246 align:middle
in evaluating the
appropriate stance of policy.

21:05.606 --> 21:06.176 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

21:06.176 --> 21:07.716 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

21:07.716 --> 21:10.166 align:middle
Madam Chair, you just said
in your opening remarks

21:11.656 --> 21:15.436 align:middle
that reducing the balance sheet
should not be "an active tool

21:15.436 --> 21:18.326 align:middle
for monetary policy in
normal times," you don't plan

21:18.326 --> 21:19.666 align:middle
to make adjustments
to the balance sheet.

21:19.666 --> 21:21.366 align:middle
I'm wondering if
we could explore

21:21.366 --> 21:24.926 align:middle
if there's any sensitivity to
the plan you just announced

21:25.476 --> 21:27.496 align:middle
if there's a spike
in interest rates,

21:27.496 --> 21:30.476 align:middle
a plunge in the stock
market, weakness in growth.

21:30.956 --> 21:32.956 align:middle
In the June statement,
you indicated

21:32.956 --> 21:37.226 align:middle
that the only reason why you
would change the-it suggested

21:37.226 --> 21:39.706 align:middle
the only reason you'd change the
balance sheet is if it required,

21:39.706 --> 21:41.896 align:middle
first, a change in
the funds rate.

21:42.516 --> 21:43.456 align:middle
Is that true?

21:43.456 --> 21:47.696 align:middle
If there's some unexpected
development in markets, or,

21:47.776 --> 21:50.916 align:middle
for example, given that we
don't know what the plans are

21:50.916 --> 21:53.756 align:middle
on the fiscal side for
the deficit in terms

21:53.756 --> 21:56.176 align:middle
of tax cuts-there
could be a sudden spike

21:56.176 --> 22:00.376 align:middle
in the deficit-will the balance
sheet reduction plan be immune

22:00.726 --> 22:01.676 align:middle
to all of that?

22:01.676 --> 22:05.006 align:middle
And, given that question,
and the idea

22:05.086 --> 22:08.756 align:middle
that this has never been done
before, why so much certainty

22:09.006 --> 22:10.366 align:middle
about the plan you've
just announced

22:10.366 --> 22:12.436 align:middle
and apparent unwillingness
to adjust it?

22:12.436 --> 22:13.056 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

22:13.556 --> 22:18.826 align:middle
So we have two policy tools
that are available to us to use:

22:18.926 --> 22:21.416 align:middle
the balance sheet
and adjustments

22:21.416 --> 22:25.246 align:middle
in short-term interest rates-our
federal funds rate target.

22:25.666 --> 22:30.026 align:middle
And, historically, the
Committee has operated

22:30.506 --> 22:36.286 align:middle
to adjust monetary conditions
to meet our economic goals

22:36.526 --> 22:38.526 align:middle
when there are shocks
to the economy

22:38.866 --> 22:42.336 align:middle
by adjusting the federal funds
rate-our short-term interest

22:42.336 --> 22:43.116 align:middle
rate target.

22:43.666 --> 22:49.376 align:middle
And that's something-a
technique of monetary control

22:49.686 --> 22:52.266 align:middle
that we have used
for a very long time

22:52.266 --> 22:53.856 align:middle
that we are familiar with,

22:54.186 --> 22:59.416 align:middle
we believe we understand pretty
well what the effects are

22:59.416 --> 23:04.196 align:middle
on the economy, market
participants understand how

23:04.196 --> 23:08.546 align:middle
that tool has been used and
would likely be adjusted

23:08.756 --> 23:11.386 align:middle
in response to shocks
to the economy.

23:11.766 --> 23:15.186 align:middle
And our preference is, when
we have two different tools

23:15.186 --> 23:19.886 align:middle
that we could use to actively
adjust the stance of policy,

23:20.286 --> 23:24.386 align:middle
to prefer and to make
a commitment that,

23:24.706 --> 23:26.896 align:middle
to the maximum extent possible,

23:27.056 --> 23:30.486 align:middle
the federal funds rate will
be the active tool of policy.

23:30.866 --> 23:32.616 align:middle
That's our go-to tool.

23:32.616 --> 23:37.986 align:middle
That is what we intend to use
unless we think that the threat

23:37.986 --> 23:43.386 align:middle
to the economy is sufficiently
great that we might have

23:43.386 --> 23:46.566 align:middle
to cut the federal funds rate-
after all, we've moved it

23:46.566 --> 23:50.716 align:middle
up to 1 to 1¼ percent
and expect it to go

23:50.716 --> 23:53.866 align:middle
up further-but a very
significant negative shock

23:53.946 --> 23:57.726 align:middle
to the economy could
conceivably force us back

23:57.726 --> 23:59.826 align:middle
to the so-called
zero lower bound.

24:00.266 --> 24:05.326 align:middle
We have said if there were that
type of material deterioration

24:05.326 --> 24:09.886 align:middle
in the outlook where we
could face a situation

24:09.886 --> 24:14.156 align:middle
where the federal funds rate
isn't a sufficient tool for us

24:14.156 --> 24:16.526 align:middle
to adjust monetary policy,

24:16.526 --> 24:22.306 align:middle
we might stop-we might stop
roll-offs from our balance sheet

24:22.306 --> 24:23.876 align:middle
and resume reinvestment.

24:24.246 --> 24:26.126 align:middle
But as long as we believe

24:26.196 --> 24:29.136 align:middle
that we can use the federal
funds rate as a tool,

24:29.416 --> 24:31.076 align:middle
that is what we intend to do.

24:31.176 --> 24:34.256 align:middle
So, if there are small
changes in the outlook

24:34.306 --> 24:38.176 align:middle
that require a recalibration
of monetary policy,

24:38.536 --> 24:42.076 align:middle
we will change our
anticipated path and setting

24:42.296 --> 24:45.246 align:middle
of the federal funds rate,
but not, for example,

24:45.296 --> 24:52.136 align:middle
change the caps on reinvestment
or stop-continue reinvestment

24:52.136 --> 24:53.996 align:middle
for a few months
and then change it.

24:54.236 --> 24:59.266 align:middle
We think that provides greater
clarity to market participants

24:59.266 --> 25:03.456 align:middle
about how policy will be
conducted and will be,

25:03.456 --> 25:06.526 align:middle
will be less confusing
and more effective

25:06.856 --> 25:08.686 align:middle
in terms of conducting policy.

25:09.066 --> 25:11.496 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

25:12.176 --> 25:13.726 align:middle
Binya Appelbaum,
the New York Times.

25:13.726 --> 25:16.056 align:middle
You have now committed
to a policy

25:16.516 --> 25:18.766 align:middle
of reducing your balance
sheet very gradually.

25:18.766 --> 25:20.726 align:middle
You have described plans

25:20.726 --> 25:23.566 align:middle
to raise interest rates even
more gradually than previously.

25:24.096 --> 25:27.296 align:middle
You are locked in for a long
period of time to a forecast

25:27.296 --> 25:30.406 align:middle
that monetary policy will
essentially keep interest rates

25:30.406 --> 25:32.756 align:middle
at a low level and keep a
balance sheet at a high level.

25:33.086 --> 25:36.806 align:middle
If something goes wrong, does
the Fed have room to respond

25:36.806 --> 25:39.166 align:middle
under these conditions in
the next several years,

25:39.496 --> 25:41.546 align:middle
and could you describe
for us what your plans are

25:41.546 --> 25:43.106 align:middle
for a response, should
it be warranted?

25:43.266 --> 25:43.926 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

25:44.266 --> 25:46.716 align:middle
So the only thing I
would object to there,

25:46.716 --> 25:50.596 align:middle
is you said that we are
"locked in," and I would say

25:50.596 --> 25:52.966 align:middle
that we are not locked in.

25:53.256 --> 25:57.426 align:middle
We believe that economic
conditions will evolve in a way

25:57.496 --> 26:00.966 align:middle
that will warrant
gradual, further increases

26:00.966 --> 26:03.096 align:middle
in our federal funds
rate target,

26:03.606 --> 26:07.496 align:middle
but if conditions evolve
differently than that,

26:08.016 --> 26:11.446 align:middle
whichever direction that
might be-it might be

26:11.446 --> 26:13.886 align:middle
that growth is more rapid,

26:13.886 --> 26:17.576 align:middle
the labor market tightens
more quickly than we assume,

26:17.576 --> 26:21.386 align:middle
and inflation appears to
be picking up more rapidly

26:21.656 --> 26:26.246 align:middle
than we had expected-we have
not promised, no matter what,

26:26.666 --> 26:30.496 align:middle
that the path of interest rate
increases will be gradual.

26:30.716 --> 26:33.826 align:middle
We believe that that
will be appropriate,

26:34.136 --> 26:37.126 align:middle
but we always watching
the economy

26:37.126 --> 26:39.476 align:middle
and will adjust policy
as appropriate.

26:39.976 --> 26:45.216 align:middle
Now, as I said, the hurdle to
changing our plans with respect

26:45.216 --> 26:48.696 align:middle
to the balance sheet,
in some sense, is high.

26:48.946 --> 26:52.196 align:middle
If conditions were to weaken,

26:52.896 --> 26:58.946 align:middle
we would really only
consider resuming reinvestment

26:59.176 --> 27:03.206 align:middle
if it were what we refer to
as a material deterioration,

27:03.526 --> 27:05.576 align:middle
and I tried to explain
why that is.

27:05.906 --> 27:11.046 align:middle
But, you know, we will
adjust monetary policy.

27:11.396 --> 27:15.906 align:middle
What you see in the dot plot is
each participant's best guess,

27:16.136 --> 27:18.476 align:middle
based on the information
they have today,

27:18.796 --> 27:23.206 align:middle
about what will be appropriate
in light of their expectations

27:23.206 --> 27:25.706 align:middle
about how the economy
would evolve.

27:25.996 --> 27:31.196 align:middle
And we think it's helpful to
show the public some sense

27:31.196 --> 27:34.766 align:middle
that it helps in understanding
our evaluation of the economy.

27:35.096 --> 27:37.036 align:middle
But we're assessing
incoming data,

27:37.036 --> 27:39.206 align:middle
and these plans are
subject to change.

27:39.486 --> 27:41.806 align:middle
What's not subject to
change is our commitment

27:41.856 --> 27:46.286 align:middle
to doing everything in our
power to achieve the goals

27:46.286 --> 27:49.716 align:middle
that Congress has assigned to
us, which are price stability

27:49.716 --> 27:52.236 align:middle
or 2 percent inflation
and maximum employment.

27:52.236 --> 27:52.906 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

27:52.906 --> 27:55.546 align:middle
Do you, in fact, have room in
the next two or three years

27:55.746 --> 27:57.296 align:middle
to respond to an
economic downturn?

27:57.756 --> 27:59.856 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

27:59.856 --> 28:04.386 align:middle
So, certainly, if growth is
stronger or if inflation picks

28:04.386 --> 28:06.346 align:middle
up more rapidly, we have room.

28:07.806 --> 28:11.596 align:middle
We have a certain
amount of room now,

28:12.316 --> 28:17.136 align:middle
and we have raised the
funds rate four times.

28:17.256 --> 28:20.386 align:middle
We believe that we are on a path

28:20.386 --> 28:23.426 align:middle
where there will likely
be further increases

28:23.646 --> 28:27.656 align:middle
over the next couple of years,
which will give us greater room,

28:29.296 --> 28:32.356 align:middle
and we think the recovery
is on a strong track.

28:32.356 --> 28:36.176 align:middle
So the reason for our actions
today in beginning to run

28:36.176 --> 28:37.746 align:middle
down the balance sheet is,

28:37.956 --> 28:40.236 align:middle
we think the economy
is performing well

28:40.756 --> 28:43.956 align:middle
and we have confidence in the
outlook for the real economy.

28:44.386 --> 28:46.216 align:middle
But, of course, there
are shocks,

28:46.696 --> 28:50.946 align:middle
and if the negative shock to
the economy were sufficient,

28:51.306 --> 28:56.736 align:middle
we recognize that
we might be unable

28:56.806 --> 29:00.816 align:middle
to pursue objectives purely by
cutting the federal funds rate,

29:01.206 --> 29:04.686 align:middle
and that is why we
say explicitly

29:04.686 --> 29:07.896 align:middle
that we would be
prepared, in that event,

29:08.006 --> 29:13.266 align:middle
to resume reinvestment, and
other tools that we used

29:13.266 --> 29:15.996 align:middle
in the financial
crisis-forward guidance-

29:16.106 --> 29:18.756 align:middle
would also be available to us.

29:20.336 --> 29:22.486 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

29:23.746 --> 29:25.486 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

29:26.146 --> 29:29.226 align:middle
Chair Yellen, Fed Governor Lael
Brainard recently gave a speech

29:29.226 --> 29:31.816 align:middle
in which she said trend
inflation appeared

29:31.816 --> 29:34.746 align:middle
to have moved lower by
around ½ percentage point.

29:35.156 --> 29:37.156 align:middle
I wanted to ask, do you agree?

29:37.716 --> 29:40.926 align:middle
And what would the Fed
need to do, if anything,

29:40.926 --> 29:43.566 align:middle
to boost trend inflation
if it has fallen?

29:43.966 --> 29:44.926 align:middle
And, related to that,

29:44.926 --> 29:48.416 align:middle
you've said you expect the
inflation softness this year

29:48.416 --> 29:49.766 align:middle
to prove "transitory."

29:50.486 --> 29:51.776 align:middle
Compared to three months ago,

29:51.776 --> 29:53.846 align:middle
how firm is your
current expectation

29:53.846 --> 29:55.986 align:middle
that the slowdown will
remain transitory,

29:56.436 --> 29:58.196 align:middle
and what implications
would that have

29:58.196 --> 30:00.616 align:middle
for monetary policy
if it is not?

30:00.656 --> 30:01.386 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

30:02.336 --> 30:03.146 align:middle
So the term

30:03.146 --> 30:07.186 align:middle
"trend inflation"-usually
there are a variety

30:07.186 --> 30:10.186 align:middle
of statistical techniques
that can be used

30:10.186 --> 30:14.056 align:middle
to extract a trend
from a series.

30:15.256 --> 30:20.496 align:middle
Exactly what that means is, in
some sense, a statistical thing,

30:20.756 --> 30:25.536 align:middle
and there are methodologies that
would show some modest decline

30:25.746 --> 30:27.506 align:middle
in recent years in the trend.

30:27.816 --> 30:30.146 align:middle
After all, we've had
a number of years

30:30.476 --> 30:32.756 align:middle
in which inflation has been low.

30:33.026 --> 30:36.436 align:middle
As I said in answer to
an earlier question,

30:36.746 --> 30:42.816 align:middle
I think if you go back to,
say, 2013 and consider the-

30:44.306 --> 30:49.596 align:middle
until this year, the reasons why
inflation was low are not hard

30:49.596 --> 30:50.656 align:middle
to understand.

30:51.956 --> 30:55.986 align:middle
It's a combination of
slack in the labor market,

30:56.306 --> 31:00.406 align:middle
declines in energy prices, and
a strong dollar that pulled

31:00.466 --> 31:02.726 align:middle
down import price inflation.

31:03.356 --> 31:06.436 align:middle
So what's important

31:06.436 --> 31:09.446 align:middle
in determining inflation
going forward is

31:09.446 --> 31:11.286 align:middle
inflation expectations.

31:12.776 --> 31:16.856 align:middle
By some-by many-by
some survey measures

31:16.856 --> 31:20.686 align:middle
of professional forecasters,
those have been rock solid.

31:21.206 --> 31:24.996 align:middle
We do also look at
household expectations,

31:24.996 --> 31:27.346 align:middle
which have come down some.

31:27.606 --> 31:30.616 align:middle
Market-based measures of
inflation compensation,

31:30.616 --> 31:33.666 align:middle
as we mentioned in the
statement-they have declined,

31:33.666 --> 31:36.086 align:middle
and they've been
stable in recent months,

31:36.136 --> 31:39.816 align:middle
but they've declined
to levels that are low

31:39.816 --> 31:41.706 align:middle
by historical standards.

31:42.736 --> 31:44.526 align:middle
That might suggest

31:44.526 --> 31:47.376 align:middle
that inflation expectations
have come down,

31:47.706 --> 31:51.626 align:middle
but one can't get a clear
read-there are risk premia built

31:51.626 --> 31:56.026 align:middle
into inflation compensation
that make it impossible

31:56.106 --> 32:00.106 align:middle
to extract directly what
inflation expectations are.

32:00.666 --> 32:05.196 align:middle
So, you know, there
is a miss this year.

32:05.246 --> 32:10.176 align:middle
I can't say I can easily point
to a sufficient set of factors

32:10.176 --> 32:14.656 align:middle
that explain this year why
inflation has been this low.

32:14.656 --> 32:17.826 align:middle
I've mentioned a few
idiosyncratic things, but,

32:18.016 --> 32:21.916 align:middle
frankly, the low inflation
is more broad based

32:21.966 --> 32:24.566 align:middle
than just idiosyncratic things.

32:25.006 --> 32:30.806 align:middle
The fact that inflation is
unusually low this year does not

32:30.806 --> 32:32.866 align:middle
mean that that's
going to continue.

32:33.346 --> 32:37.036 align:middle
Remember that in
January and February,

32:37.106 --> 32:40.236 align:middle
core inflation was running
over a 12-month basis

32:40.536 --> 32:45.216 align:middle
at around 1.9 percent, and we
look to be very close to 2 now.

32:45.476 --> 32:48.966 align:middle
We've had several months of data
that have meaningfully pulled

32:49.286 --> 32:54.156 align:middle
that down, and what we need
to do is figure out whether

32:54.156 --> 32:58.756 align:middle
or not the factors that have
lowered inflation are likely

32:58.756 --> 33:03.876 align:middle
to prove persistent or they're
likely to prove transitory,

33:04.156 --> 33:07.346 align:middle
and that's what we're going
to try to be determining

33:07.346 --> 33:09.786 align:middle
on the basis of incoming data.

33:09.786 --> 33:13.426 align:middle
And you asked me about
the policy implications.

33:13.906 --> 33:17.646 align:middle
Of course, if we
determined our view changed,

33:17.986 --> 33:21.516 align:middle
and instead of thinking that
the factors holding inflation

33:21.516 --> 33:25.266 align:middle
down were transitory,
we came to the view

33:25.266 --> 33:28.936 align:middle
that they would be persistent,
it would require an alteration

33:28.936 --> 33:33.786 align:middle
in monetary policy to move
inflation back up to 2 percent,

33:33.786 --> 33:36.296 align:middle
and we would be committed
to making that adjustment.

33:36.866 --> 33:38.146 align:middle
ANN SAPHIR.

33:42.226 --> 33:44.036 align:middle
Hi, Ann Saphir with Reuters.

33:44.336 --> 33:47.656 align:middle
I wanted to ask you,
markets seem to be pricing

33:47.656 --> 33:52.756 align:middle
in still a shallower path of
rate hikes than the Fed does

33:52.756 --> 33:56.396 align:middle
in the SEP, and I
wonder, what do you think

33:56.396 --> 33:59.386 align:middle
that markets might be
missing here, and, sort of,

33:59.386 --> 34:01.626 align:middle
what's your conviction
about, you know,

34:02.116 --> 34:08.036 align:middle
that your view is the correct
one on the gradual-the pace that

34:08.036 --> 34:11.196 align:middle
"gradual" means, which seems
to be a little bit faster

34:11.196 --> 34:13.066 align:middle
than what markets are pricing?

34:13.406 --> 34:14.056 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

34:14.056 --> 34:16.676 align:middle
So I'm not really going to try

34:16.676 --> 34:19.546 align:middle
to explain what market
participants are thinking.

34:19.546 --> 34:21.916 align:middle
I think all of us-both market

34:21.916 --> 34:27.476 align:middle
and FOMC participants'
paths have come down.

34:27.756 --> 34:30.046 align:middle
Not in the last couple
of quarters,

34:30.116 --> 34:32.376 align:middle
but over the last several years,

34:32.766 --> 34:35.826 align:middle
there's been a growing
recognition

34:35.896 --> 34:39.466 align:middle
that the so-called
neutral interest rate,

34:39.746 --> 34:44.156 align:middle
consistent with the economy
operating at maximum employment,

34:44.616 --> 34:47.566 align:middle
that that rate seems
to have come down,

34:47.566 --> 34:53.776 align:middle
and most of the economic
papers and research bearing

34:53.776 --> 34:56.736 align:middle
on this topic suggest
that it's quite low.

34:58.826 --> 35:04.876 align:middle
Market-SEP-the FOMC
participants-you can see

35:04.876 --> 35:10.996 align:middle
by their estimates of the
longer-run normal rate

35:10.996 --> 35:15.986 align:middle
of interest, it is-this time it
came down from-the median came

35:15.986 --> 35:19.976 align:middle
down from 3 percent
to 2.75 percent.

35:20.466 --> 35:25.116 align:middle
So that shows that even in the
long run, FOMC participants,

35:25.386 --> 35:29.146 align:middle
in light of incoming data,
are adjusting their views.

35:29.416 --> 35:33.766 align:middle
I would say they still
believe that, in real terms,

35:33.976 --> 35:36.616 align:middle
that neutral rate will
be rising somewhat

35:36.946 --> 35:38.726 align:middle
over the next few years.

35:39.026 --> 35:43.366 align:middle
With a 2 percent inflation
rate, that long-run estimate

35:44.036 --> 35:48.556 align:middle
in real terms amounts to 75
basis points, which is higher

35:48.556 --> 35:52.466 align:middle
than the zero or slightly
positive rates now.

35:52.806 --> 36:00.056 align:middle
So that's one factor that
explains the path in the SEP.

36:00.136 --> 36:06.026 align:middle
Market participants may have
lower estimates or believe

36:06.146 --> 36:09.306 align:middle
that a low neutral rate
may be more persistent.

36:09.556 --> 36:12.886 align:middle
I mean, let me emphasize
that, as I said before,

36:12.886 --> 36:16.096 align:middle
there's nothing set in
stone about the policy paths

36:16.536 --> 36:20.526 align:middle
that you see in the
summary of projections.

36:20.526 --> 36:22.936 align:middle
There's a great deal of
uncertainty around them.

36:23.216 --> 36:26.286 align:middle
Not only is there disagreement,
there's also uncertainty,

36:26.646 --> 36:32.446 align:middle
and FOMC participants have been
revising their views over time,

36:32.746 --> 36:34.506 align:middle
and they will continue to do so.

36:34.896 --> 36:37.016 align:middle
I'd also point out a couple

36:37.016 --> 36:41.506 align:middle
of technical reasons
why it's difficult

36:41.606 --> 36:48.056 align:middle
to compare what you see in the
SEP and market-implied paths.

36:48.376 --> 36:52.746 align:middle
One is that FOMC
participants are writing

36:52.746 --> 36:56.296 align:middle
down what they think
is the most likely,

36:56.296 --> 36:59.556 align:middle
or modal, outcome for rates.

37:00.186 --> 37:02.706 align:middle
But, of course, there
are downside risks.

37:02.996 --> 37:07.896 align:middle
And the mean rate, if they
were asked to write that down,

37:07.956 --> 37:11.086 align:middle
would take account of
weighing all possible outcomes

37:11.336 --> 37:15.416 align:middle
and likely would be lower than
what participants are writing

37:15.416 --> 37:17.536 align:middle
down as their most
likely outcome.

37:17.946 --> 37:25.106 align:middle
In addition, in markets, many
economists have suggested

37:25.106 --> 37:30.706 align:middle
that there are term premia
that can affect moving

37:30.706 --> 37:34.766 align:middle
from the so-called
market-implied path to the view

37:34.766 --> 37:37.756 align:middle
for what the future
federal funds rate path is,

37:38.136 --> 37:41.186 align:middle
and if those term
premia are negative,

37:41.186 --> 37:43.816 align:middle
as many economists
think they likely are,

37:44.086 --> 37:47.606 align:middle
there's a little bit less
difference between what you see

37:48.086 --> 37:51.126 align:middle
in the FOMC plot and
the market-implied plot.

37:51.606 --> 37:53.116 align:middle
JIM PUZZANGHERA.

37:56.116 --> 37:57.996 align:middle
Thanks. Jim Puzzanghera
with the L.A. Times.

37:57.996 --> 38:01.156 align:middle
Chair Yellen, your term
expires as Chair in February.

38:01.406 --> 38:05.756 align:middle
Have you had a chance to meet
with or discuss your situation

38:05.756 --> 38:07.416 align:middle
with President Trump yet?

38:07.416 --> 38:10.016 align:middle
And if so, what were
your impressions of him

38:10.016 --> 38:12.356 align:middle
and what he's looking for
from the Federal Reserve?

38:13.256 --> 38:13.686 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

38:13.826 --> 38:18.306 align:middle
So I have said that I intend
to serve out my term as Chair,

38:19.236 --> 38:23.656 align:middle
and that I'm really
not going to comment

38:23.656 --> 38:26.086 align:middle
on my intentions beyond that.

38:26.086 --> 38:28.866 align:middle
I will say that I have
not had a further meeting

38:28.866 --> 38:29.926 align:middle
with President Trump.

38:30.296 --> 38:32.016 align:middle
I met with him early in my term,

38:32.016 --> 38:38.146 align:middle
and I've not had a further
meeting with him.1 ADAM SHAPIRO.

38:38.216 --> 38:39.736 align:middle
Adam Shapiro, Fox Business.

38:39.736 --> 38:43.326 align:middle
Chair Yellen, a month ago you
delivered a speech in Wyoming

38:43.326 --> 38:45.506 align:middle
in which you said, "The
balance of research suggests

38:45.506 --> 38:46.836 align:middle
that the core reforms
we have put

38:46.836 --> 38:49.516 align:middle
in place have substantially
boosted resilience

38:49.516 --> 38:51.796 align:middle
without unduly limiting
credit availability

38:51.796 --> 38:52.776 align:middle
or economic growth."

38:52.806 --> 38:54.796 align:middle
I have a two-part question
based on that quote.

38:54.866 --> 38:59.536 align:middle
First, 1 Chair Yellen
intended to say that she met

38:59.536 --> 39:02.476 align:middle
with President Trump
early in his term.

39:02.476 --> 39:06.006 align:middle
what message do you want
Congress and President Trump

39:06.006 --> 39:08.286 align:middle
to hear from that statement?

39:08.286 --> 39:10.866 align:middle
And then, regarding
economic growth,

39:10.996 --> 39:14.116 align:middle
the accommodative process
that the Fed has followed

39:14.116 --> 39:18.726 align:middle
for the last 10 years has helped
bring us to full employment,

39:18.726 --> 39:23.156 align:middle
but economists point out
that there have been people

39:23.156 --> 39:24.756 align:middle
who haven't benefited.

39:24.756 --> 39:28.456 align:middle
For instance, 52 percent
of Americans own stock,

39:28.846 --> 39:32.126 align:middle
48 percent don't-they've
not participated

39:32.126 --> 39:35.816 align:middle
in the gains in the
stock market.

39:36.036 --> 39:40.946 align:middle
Housing prices-the median house
price is now at a record high,

39:41.176 --> 39:47.146 align:middle
and 39 million Americans,
according to a Harvard study,

39:47.146 --> 39:50.116 align:middle
spend more than 30
percent for housing.

39:50.166 --> 39:53.266 align:middle
So what would you say to those
people about Fed policies

39:53.266 --> 39:56.676 align:middle
and the impact they've
had on their lives?

39:56.676 --> 39:57.466 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

39:57.466 --> 40:00.956 align:middle
Okay. So you asked me,
what was the main-first,

40:01.096 --> 40:04.536 align:middle
what was the main message of
my speech, and I would say it's

40:04.536 --> 40:08.306 align:middle
that we put in place,
since the financial crisis,

40:08.306 --> 40:12.416 align:middle
a set of core reforms that
have strengthened the financial

40:12.416 --> 40:15.406 align:middle
system, and, in my
personal view,

40:15.406 --> 40:16.756 align:middle
it's important they
remain in place.

40:17.006 --> 40:20.956 align:middle
And those core reforms are more
capital, higher-quality capital,

40:21.046 --> 40:22.866 align:middle
more liquidity-especially

40:22.866 --> 40:28.206 align:middle
in systemically important
banking institutions-stress

40:28.206 --> 40:30.196 align:middle
testing, and resolution plans.

40:30.196 --> 40:32.986 align:middle
And those four prongs
of improvements

40:32.986 --> 40:35.336 align:middle
in banking supervision have
really strengthened the

40:35.336 --> 40:37.856 align:middle
financial system and
made it more resilient,

40:38.096 --> 40:39.616 align:middle
and I believe they
should stay in place.

40:40.256 --> 40:44.416 align:middle
But I also tried to
emphasize-and I believe

40:44.416 --> 40:48.866 align:middle
that they have contributed
to growth

40:48.866 --> 40:51.196 align:middle
and the availability of credit.

40:51.196 --> 40:52.816 align:middle
I've also tried to emphasize

40:52.896 --> 40:55.226 align:middle
that all regulators
should be attentive

40:55.226 --> 40:59.336 align:middle
to undue regulatory
burden and look for ways

40:59.336 --> 41:00.256 align:middle
to try to scale that back.

41:00.256 --> 41:01.696 align:middle
And this is especially
true after years

41:02.066 --> 41:04.796 align:middle
in which we have
implemented a large number

41:05.286 --> 41:07.036 align:middle
of complex regulations,

41:07.036 --> 41:08.696 align:middle
and we have been
committed to doing that.

41:08.796 --> 41:12.516 align:middle
I would point out
particularly community banks

41:12.516 --> 41:16.716 align:middle
that are laboring under
significant regulatory burden.

41:16.716 --> 41:19.686 align:middle
We have been looking for
ways to scale back burdens,

41:19.826 --> 41:23.696 align:middle
running the EGRPRA process,
where we've listened

41:23.696 --> 41:28.816 align:middle
to concerns among community
banks and are looking for ways,

41:28.816 --> 41:33.156 align:middle
for example, to simplify capital
standards and reduce burdens,

41:33.386 --> 41:35.676 align:middle
and that's very important.

41:35.976 --> 41:41.436 align:middle
More generally, we want to
tailor-we want to, and we would

41:41.436 --> 41:47.116 align:middle
like to see Congress as
well-we can do things

41:47.146 --> 41:51.186 align:middle
to appropriately tailor
regulations to the risk posed

41:51.186 --> 41:53.766 align:middle
by different kinds of
banking organizations.

41:54.136 --> 41:59.316 align:middle
There are some things that
Congress could also do to help

41:59.316 --> 42:03.096 align:middle
that process, and we have made
some concrete suggestions.

42:03.096 --> 42:07.256 align:middle
And then some of the regulations
that we have put in place

42:07.256 --> 42:11.306 align:middle
with other regulators since the
crisis, like the Volcker rule,

42:11.466 --> 42:13.496 align:middle
are really quite complex,

42:13.496 --> 42:17.206 align:middle
and we're working-we believe
we should-and we're working

42:17.206 --> 42:22.676 align:middle
with other regulators to try
to see if we can find ways,

42:22.936 --> 42:27.536 align:middle
while carrying out what
Dodd-Frank intended,

42:27.536 --> 42:30.646 align:middle
that banking organizations
not be involved

42:30.646 --> 42:32.296 align:middle
in proprietary trading.

42:32.706 --> 42:36.766 align:middle
Nevertheless, the implementation
can be less complex.

42:37.116 --> 42:40.636 align:middle
So that was my main message.

42:41.456 --> 42:46.826 align:middle
Your second question asked about
what impact the Fed has had

42:46.826 --> 42:51.786 align:middle
on income distribution
because of the fact that stocks

42:51.786 --> 42:54.896 align:middle
and homes tend to be
disproportionately owned.

42:54.896 --> 42:59.886 align:middle
So, I would say, look, we were
faced with a huge recession

42:59.886 --> 43:05.616 align:middle
that took an enormous toll in
terms of depriving large numbers

43:05.616 --> 43:09.796 align:middle
of people-and disproportionately
lower-income people

43:10.296 --> 43:11.726 align:middle
who are less advantaged

43:11.726 --> 43:14.686 align:middle
in the labor market-found
themselves without work.

43:15.156 --> 43:17.546 align:middle
We had a 10 percent
unemployment rate,

43:17.546 --> 43:21.506 align:middle
and our congressional
mandate is maximum employment

43:21.506 --> 43:22.626 align:middle
and price stability.

43:23.016 --> 43:25.766 align:middle
So we set monetary policy not

43:25.766 --> 43:30.566 align:middle
with a view toward affecting
the distribution of income,

43:30.566 --> 43:34.436 align:middle
but toward pursuing those
congressionally mandated goals.

43:34.836 --> 43:38.426 align:middle
And I am pleased to see
the unemployment rate

43:38.426 --> 43:42.256 align:middle
and every other measure
that I know of pertaining

43:42.256 --> 43:45.706 align:middle
to the labor market show
dramatic improvement

43:45.706 --> 43:50.026 align:middle
over these years, and
that is hugely important

43:50.026 --> 43:56.286 align:middle
to the economic well-being not
at the top end of the wealth

43:56.286 --> 43:59.676 align:middle
and income distribution,
but to the bottom end

43:59.676 --> 44:01.286 align:middle
of the income distribution.

44:01.286 --> 44:04.106 align:middle
And we have seen this
year median income

44:04.106 --> 44:06.636 align:middle
in real terms rise
significantly,

44:06.636 --> 44:09.106 align:middle
with gains throughout
the income distribution.

44:10.206 --> 44:11.466 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

44:13.496 --> 44:15.116 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger,
Associated Press.

44:15.956 --> 44:18.356 align:middle
Madam Chair, the next
month, with the departure

44:18.356 --> 44:21.916 align:middle
of Vice Chair Fischer,
the Fed's going

44:21.916 --> 44:24.126 align:middle
to lose its quorum-the
Fed Board-you'll go

44:24.126 --> 44:25.066 align:middle
to three members.

44:25.566 --> 44:26.926 align:middle
Do you have-is that going

44:26.926 --> 44:29.336 align:middle
to present operational
challenges for you?

44:29.336 --> 44:30.946 align:middle
Do have contingency plans?

44:31.406 --> 44:32.646 align:middle
Has the Senate assured you

44:32.646 --> 44:37.976 align:middle
that Mr. Quarles will be-his
confirmation will be approved?

44:38.406 --> 44:40.866 align:middle
And in your discussions
with the Administration,

44:40.866 --> 44:44.816 align:middle
have they given you any
assurances that the pace

44:44.816 --> 44:47.576 align:middle
of nominations is
going to pick up soon?

44:47.576 --> 44:47.896 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

44:48.466 --> 44:53.006 align:middle
So first let me say that I will
greatly miss Vice Chair Fischer.

44:53.006 --> 44:57.486 align:middle
He's made enormous
contributions to the FOMC

44:57.486 --> 45:00.766 align:middle
and to the broader work
of the Federal Reserve,

45:00.766 --> 45:03.886 align:middle
and I have really
enjoyed working with him

45:03.886 --> 45:08.686 align:middle
and appreciated his wise
counsel and friendship.

45:09.316 --> 45:14.406 align:middle
It is conceivable that we will
be down to three Governors.

45:14.636 --> 45:19.326 align:middle
I have full confidence that even
if that happens, we will be able

45:19.326 --> 45:24.876 align:middle
to carry out our complement
of responsibilities.

45:25.886 --> 45:30.286 align:middle
There's-every action that
we are allowed to take

45:30.286 --> 45:33.356 align:middle
under the Federal
Reserve Act can be taken,

45:33.586 --> 45:40.286 align:middle
even if we are a Board of three,
although we will have to abide,

45:40.286 --> 45:44.216 align:middle
as we always do, by the
restrictions that are part

45:44.216 --> 45:47.016 align:middle
of the Government
in the Sunshine Act.

45:47.016 --> 45:53.036 align:middle
I would welcome a full
complement of colleagues.

45:53.736 --> 45:56.176 align:middle
We have a lot of work to
do, and it would be nice

45:56.176 --> 45:58.366 align:middle
to distribute it
over more people.

45:58.676 --> 46:00.836 align:middle
But, perhaps more
important than that,

46:01.216 --> 46:05.206 align:middle
I think it's very important
to have a broad range of views

46:05.206 --> 46:09.736 align:middle
around the table as we
deliberate on policy actions.

46:10.046 --> 46:14.136 align:middle
I've had very good
interactions with Randy Quarles

46:14.136 --> 46:17.026 align:middle
and hope he will be confirmed.

46:17.026 --> 46:20.516 align:middle
I look forward to working
with him, and, you know,

46:21.056 --> 46:24.506 align:middle
I hope that the Administration
will make other nominations

46:24.506 --> 46:25.446 align:middle
to fill our slots.

46:25.886 --> 46:30.176 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

46:30.296 --> 46:32.506 align:middle
Heather Long from
the Washington Post.

46:32.636 --> 46:35.326 align:middle
As you know, Congress is
considering a major tax

46:35.326 --> 46:36.476 align:middle
reform package.

46:36.566 --> 46:39.366 align:middle
Do you or any of the
Committee members have concerns

46:39.416 --> 46:43.316 align:middle
if that package does not end up
being deficit neutral and ends

46:43.316 --> 46:44.426 align:middle
up adding to the debt?

46:44.976 --> 46:46.866 align:middle
Would that be problematic
for the economy?

46:47.726 --> 46:48.156 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

46:48.256 --> 46:53.186 align:middle
Look, that's something
that's a matter for Congress

46:53.186 --> 46:55.436 align:middle
and the White House to decide.

46:58.216 --> 47:03.656 align:middle
You know, I've put forward a few
principles about fiscal policy

47:03.706 --> 47:08.716 align:middle
that I would reiterate,
that one of the problems

47:08.716 --> 47:11.426 align:middle
that the American
economy suffers from,

47:11.666 --> 47:15.166 align:middle
along with many other
economies around the globe,

47:15.166 --> 47:17.186 align:middle
is slow productivity growth,

47:17.616 --> 47:20.306 align:middle
and I think it would
be very desirable

47:20.306 --> 47:24.526 align:middle
if a fiscal package
had the potential in it

47:24.526 --> 47:28.806 align:middle
to create incentives that would
raise productivity growth.

47:29.306 --> 47:33.746 align:middle
We do face, in terms
of longer-term deficits

47:33.986 --> 47:39.076 align:middle
as the population ages,
an unsustainable debt path

47:39.476 --> 47:43.946 align:middle
that will require, I
believe, some adjustments

47:43.946 --> 47:47.806 align:middle
to fiscal policy, and I hope
Congress will keep that in mind.

47:47.806 --> 47:50.666 align:middle
But beyond a few
core principles,

47:50.666 --> 47:53.306 align:middle
it's really-I don't want
to weigh in on details.

47:53.656 --> 47:56.666 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

47:56.666 --> 47:58.626 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
from Marketplace.

47:58.896 --> 48:01.876 align:middle
When you testified
before Congress last July,

48:02.796 --> 48:04.806 align:middle
you said that you
might be prepared

48:04.806 --> 48:07.586 align:middle
to take enforcement
actions against Wells Fargo

48:08.026 --> 48:09.916 align:middle
if it proved to be appropriate.

48:10.276 --> 48:13.036 align:middle
Do you think it's appropriate,
and what actions could you take?

48:14.016 --> 48:14.886 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

48:15.016 --> 48:18.246 align:middle
So let me say that I
consider the behavior

48:18.556 --> 48:20.926 align:middle
of Wells Fargo towards
its customers

48:20.926 --> 48:24.576 align:middle
to have been egregious
and unacceptable.

48:25.116 --> 48:28.866 align:middle
We take our supervision
responsibilities

48:28.866 --> 48:35.896 align:middle
at the company very
seriously, and we are attempting

48:35.896 --> 48:39.436 align:middle
to understand what
the root causes

48:39.436 --> 48:43.066 align:middle
of those problems are
and to address them.

48:43.466 --> 48:49.096 align:middle
I'm not able to discuss
confidential supervisory

48:49.096 --> 48:52.396 align:middle
information and not yet able

48:52.466 --> 48:56.646 align:middle
to tell you what actions we
may take, but I do want to say

48:56.646 --> 49:00.586 align:middle
that we are committed to
taking the actions we regard

49:00.586 --> 49:07.116 align:middle
as necessary and appropriate
to make sure that the right set

49:07.116 --> 49:09.786 align:middle
of controls are in place
in that organization.

49:10.636 --> 49:11.686 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

49:11.686 --> 49:13.076 align:middle
Can you give me any
kind of a timeline?

49:13.566 --> 49:14.236 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

49:14.236 --> 49:16.016 align:middle
We are working very hard on it.

49:16.016 --> 49:16.646 align:middle
DAVID HARRISON.

49:17.886 --> 49:20.616 align:middle
Hi, thank you.

49:20.886 --> 49:22.436 align:middle
David Harrison with Dow Jones.

49:22.436 --> 49:25.186 align:middle
I'd like to follow up on
the balance sheet question,

49:25.186 --> 49:25.786 align:middle
if I may.

49:26.866 --> 49:31.786 align:middle
What specifically would it take
for you to reverse the decision

49:31.786 --> 49:33.196 align:middle
to wind down the balance sheet,

49:33.196 --> 49:36.406 align:middle
and under what conditions
would you consider adding

49:36.406 --> 49:37.466 align:middle
to the balance sheet again?

49:37.906 --> 49:41.966 align:middle
And separately, as a follow-up
to that, looking more broadly,

49:42.316 --> 49:44.836 align:middle
how do you think history
will judge the effectiveness

49:44.836 --> 49:48.876 align:middle
of your asset purchases and
the conditions under which

49:48.876 --> 49:51.176 align:middle
that policy should be used?

49:51.336 --> 49:51.576 align:middle
Thank you.

49:51.986 --> 49:54.256 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

49:54.636 --> 49:59.056 align:middle
So, starting with the last
part of the question, I mean,

49:59.056 --> 50:04.336 align:middle
my own judgment,
based on my experience

50:04.336 --> 50:08.656 align:middle
and the economic
research that has tried

50:08.656 --> 50:10.656 align:middle
to estimate the effectiveness

50:10.656 --> 50:15.676 align:middle
of our balance sheet
actions starting in 2008

50:15.946 --> 50:20.766 align:middle
and has also looked at the
similar balance sheet actions

50:21.056 --> 50:24.156 align:middle
in other parts of the world,
including the euro area,

50:24.676 --> 50:27.846 align:middle
is that these actions
were successful

50:28.176 --> 50:34.036 align:middle
in making financial conditions
more accommodative and,

50:34.356 --> 50:37.206 align:middle
I believe, in stimulating
a faster recovery

50:37.206 --> 50:40.256 align:middle
than we otherwise
would have had.

50:40.256 --> 50:46.816 align:middle
A recent Fed working paper
estimated that the full set

50:46.816 --> 50:52.496 align:middle
of balance sheet actions that we
took during the crisis may have

50:52.786 --> 50:56.846 align:middle
lowered long-term interest
rates by about 100 basis points.

50:57.396 --> 51:00.756 align:middle
Obviously, there are
different-there are different

51:00.836 --> 51:04.086 align:middle
estimates around-of
what difference it made,

51:04.086 --> 51:07.126 align:middle
but I would say that
it's effective.

51:07.776 --> 51:12.436 align:middle
It will be up to future
policymakers to decide,

51:13.216 --> 51:15.676 align:middle
in the event of a
severe downturn,

51:16.126 --> 51:20.996 align:middle
whether they think it's
appropriate to again resort

51:20.996 --> 51:26.946 align:middle
to balance sheet-to adding
assets to a balance sheet.

51:27.556 --> 51:34.326 align:middle
I would say that if economists
are correct that we're living

51:34.326 --> 51:38.866 align:middle
in a world where the level of
neutral interest rates not only

51:38.866 --> 51:45.266 align:middle
in the United States, but around
the world is likely to be low

51:45.266 --> 51:49.246 align:middle
in the future due to
slow productivity growth

51:49.246 --> 51:52.916 align:middle
and demographics-now, we don't
know that that view will bear

51:52.916 --> 51:58.416 align:middle
out to be correct, but it is
a view that many people adhere

51:58.416 --> 52:01.006 align:middle
to when there is evidence of it-

52:01.376 --> 52:06.636 align:middle
then future policymakers will
be faced with the question of,

52:06.966 --> 52:11.836 align:middle
in the event of a severe
downturn where they're not able

52:11.836 --> 52:15.416 align:middle
to provide as much stimulus
as they would ideally

52:15.416 --> 52:18.826 align:middle
like by cutting overnight
interest rates,

52:19.266 --> 52:23.766 align:middle
what other actions
are available to them?

52:23.766 --> 52:28.656 align:middle
And during the crisis, we
bought longer-term assets

52:28.656 --> 52:33.136 align:middle
and used forward guidance, and
for my own part, I would want

52:33.136 --> 52:37.316 align:middle
to keep those things in the
toolkit as being available.

52:37.796 --> 52:41.666 align:middle
It will be up to future
policymakers to decide how

52:41.666 --> 52:45.176 align:middle
to rank those, or whether or
not there might be other options

52:45.516 --> 52:46.926 align:middle
that are available to them,

52:46.926 --> 52:50.456 align:middle
but I don't think this
issue will go away,

52:50.456 --> 52:52.566 align:middle
although perhaps
it's only- well,

52:53.296 --> 52:57.576 align:middle
this could well be a decision

52:57.576 --> 53:01.596 align:middle
that future policymakers will
have to face in the event

53:01.596 --> 53:05.356 align:middle
of a significant
asset-economic shock.

53:05.356 --> 53:09.046 align:middle
I mean, you asked me
what would it take for us

53:09.116 --> 53:15.626 align:middle
to resume reinvestment, and
I can't really say much more

53:15.626 --> 53:18.776 align:middle
than we said in the
guidance that we provided,

53:18.776 --> 53:24.366 align:middle
which is that if there is
a material deterioration

53:24.366 --> 53:28.536 align:middle
in the economic outlook and
we thought we might be faced

53:28.536 --> 53:32.726 align:middle
with a situation
where we would need

53:32.726 --> 53:37.436 align:middle
to substantially cut the federal
funds rate and could be limited

53:37.436 --> 53:41.596 align:middle
by the so-called zero lower
bound, it is that type

53:41.646 --> 53:48.536 align:middle
of determination that our
Committee is saying would-might

53:48.536 --> 53:51.836 align:middle
lead us to resume reinvestment.

53:52.466 --> 53:57.736 align:middle
So that's-our Committee
has been unanimous

53:57.736 --> 54:03.196 align:middle
in affirming this statement
of intentions, so, you know,

54:03.196 --> 54:06.466 align:middle
I think that's where
our Committee stands.

54:06.466 --> 54:10.756 align:middle
So that is a somewhat high
bar to resume reinvestments,

54:10.756 --> 54:13.716 align:middle
and that's why, in answering
previous questions, I would say,

54:13.716 --> 54:18.166 align:middle
well, you know, if there's
some small, negative shock,

54:18.436 --> 54:21.036 align:middle
our first tool-our
most important

54:21.036 --> 54:23.946 align:middle
and reliable tool-will be
the federal funds rate.

54:23.996 --> 54:28.256 align:middle
But if there is a significant
shock that's a material

54:28.256 --> 54:30.386 align:middle
deterioration to the outlook,

54:30.626 --> 54:33.416 align:middle
we would consider
resuming reinvestment.

54:34.516 --> 54:36.086 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

54:37.376 --> 54:39.106 align:middle
Hi, Chair Yellen.

54:39.106 --> 54:40.846 align:middle
Victoria Guida with Politico.

54:41.456 --> 54:44.336 align:middle
You've been on the Financial
Stability Oversight Council

54:44.336 --> 54:46.406 align:middle
for a few years now,
and I was wondering

54:46.406 --> 54:49.406 align:middle
if you had any thoughts on
whether the designation process

54:49.406 --> 54:53.146 align:middle
for Systemically Important
Financial Institutions should be

54:53.146 --> 54:54.876 align:middle
changed or improved in any way.

54:55.366 --> 55:00.936 align:middle
And a somewhat separate, but
related, question: The situation

55:00.936 --> 55:04.986 align:middle
with Equifax-I was wondering if
there's anything related to that

55:05.156 --> 55:08.746 align:middle
that might raise systemic issues
that FSOC would need to discuss.

55:09.326 --> 55:09.646 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

55:12.976 --> 55:16.446 align:middle
So you asked first about
the designation process.

55:18.406 --> 55:23.136 align:middle
So during the time that
I've been on the FSOC,

55:23.716 --> 55:27.956 align:middle
only one firm was-several
firms were designated before

55:27.956 --> 55:28.876 align:middle
I participated.

55:29.266 --> 55:33.516 align:middle
MetLife was designated during
the time that I was there,

55:33.516 --> 55:37.326 align:middle
and I have seen how the
designation process works.

55:37.736 --> 55:42.906 align:middle
I do think designation
is important.

55:42.906 --> 55:45.386 align:middle
We saw during the
financial crisis

55:45.386 --> 55:51.266 align:middle
that some systemically important
nonbanking organizations

55:51.266 --> 55:57.286 align:middle
like Lehman and AIG-that their
distress produced broad systemic

55:57.726 --> 56:02.166 align:middle
consequences that were
adverse for the U.S. economy.

56:02.576 --> 56:07.636 align:middle
And having the ability
to designate firms

56:07.976 --> 56:11.826 align:middle
when the FSOC makes
the determination

56:12.136 --> 56:17.146 align:middle
that their distress or
failure could have systemic

56:17.146 --> 56:22.546 align:middle
repercussions, I believe that's
an important-that's an important

56:22.546 --> 56:27.176 align:middle
policy tool for FSOC
to have available.

56:27.596 --> 56:33.996 align:middle
Now, it's not meant to be a
one-way street, in the sense

56:33.996 --> 56:39.256 align:middle
that a firm that's
designated-the procedures

56:39.256 --> 56:41.876 align:middle
require annual reviews.

56:44.666 --> 56:47.066 align:middle
Firms may change
their business models

56:47.066 --> 56:50.156 align:middle
or just how they
conduct their business,

56:50.546 --> 56:55.396 align:middle
and we should welcome
de-designation of firms

56:55.396 --> 56:59.346 align:middle
if their business model is
changed in a way that leads us

56:59.636 --> 57:01.966 align:middle
to believe that their failure

57:01.966 --> 57:05.606 align:middle
or distress would no longer
be systemically important

57:05.606 --> 57:08.046 align:middle
and those are decisions
that we make every year.

57:08.396 --> 57:11.656 align:middle
So, you know, I'm satisfied
with that process so far.

57:11.856 --> 57:15.766 align:middle
GE Capital dramatically
changed its business model

57:15.766 --> 57:18.106 align:middle
and was de-designated,
and, you know,

57:18.106 --> 57:20.566 align:middle
I believe it's a
process that works.

57:20.796 --> 57:24.076 align:middle
So I know that the
Treasury is looking at this

57:24.076 --> 57:27.746 align:middle
and may make recommendations,
and if they do, I'd be,

57:27.746 --> 57:31.106 align:middle
you know, glad to
consider them as, you know,

57:31.106 --> 57:34.556 align:middle
part of the FSOC process, but
I think it's been important

57:34.556 --> 57:35.696 align:middle
and basically working.

57:36.326 --> 57:38.656 align:middle
You asked about Equifax
and, you know,

57:38.656 --> 57:42.326 align:middle
of course that is a very
serious data breach.

57:42.706 --> 57:48.976 align:middle
We would really urge consumers
now to be very careful

57:48.976 --> 57:53.886 align:middle
in monitoring their credit
reports and financial situation.

57:54.246 --> 57:58.186 align:middle
And, through our supervision,
we're working with the banks

57:58.676 --> 58:01.076 align:middle
that we supervise to make sure

58:01.076 --> 58:05.646 align:middle
that they take appropriate
actions with respect

58:05.746 --> 58:09.396 align:middle
to their business processes
in light of the fact

58:09.786 --> 58:15.006 align:middle
that there could be breaches
or fraudulent transactions

58:15.006 --> 58:17.406 align:middle
or information that
they received

58:17.406 --> 58:19.086 align:middle
that they might use-for example,

58:19.086 --> 58:26.476 align:middle
in credit determinations-could
be contaminated by bad data.

58:26.476 --> 58:30.276 align:middle
More generally, it
points to the importance

58:30.596 --> 58:34.746 align:middle
of strong cybersecurity
controls and attention

58:34.746 --> 58:38.656 align:middle
to cybersecurity risks,
which we do see as one

58:38.656 --> 58:42.856 align:middle
of the most significant risks
to the financial sector.

58:42.856 --> 58:46.236 align:middle
And we are very focused
in our banking supervision

58:46.556 --> 58:51.796 align:middle
in making sure that banks have
appropriate controls in place.

58:51.796 --> 58:53.906 align:middle
And the Equifax breach, I think,

58:54.166 --> 58:56.266 align:middle
highlights the importance
of that.

58:57.276 --> 58:59.116 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

58:59.796 --> 59:02.056 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

59:02.636 --> 59:05.176 align:middle
We've talked a lot today
about what you're going to do

59:05.176 --> 59:07.756 align:middle
and what you may do, but
not necessarily about why.

59:08.696 --> 59:10.696 align:middle
With four rate increases
behind you,

59:10.696 --> 59:14.306 align:middle
financial conditions are
looser than before you began.

59:14.306 --> 59:16.766 align:middle
And I'm wondering if that
bothers you, if you're concerned

59:16.766 --> 59:18.876 align:middle
about overstimulating
the economy,

59:18.936 --> 59:23.176 align:middle
or if you feel inflation could
break out much more quickly

59:23.176 --> 59:26.376 align:middle
than we have seen, or if
you feel there's a financial

59:26.376 --> 59:28.676 align:middle
stability question
because stocks, bonds,

59:28.676 --> 59:30.946 align:middle
and real estate are
all so expensive now.

59:30.946 --> 59:33.516 align:middle
How would you explain
what the Fed is doing,

59:33.776 --> 59:35.826 align:middle
why the Fed is doing it,
to the American people?

59:36.586 --> 59:37.096 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

59:37.336 --> 59:41.106 align:middle
So, first of all, let me
say that the decisions

59:41.106 --> 59:44.746 align:middle
that we've made this year
about rates and today

59:44.746 --> 59:48.286 align:middle
about our balance sheet
are ones we've taken

59:48.286 --> 59:51.606 align:middle
because we feel the U.S.
economy is performing well.

59:52.496 --> 59:57.056 align:middle
We are working down
our balance sheet

59:57.196 --> 01:00:01.476 align:middle
because we feel that's stimulus

01:00:01.736 --> 01:00:04.096 align:middle
that in some sense
is no longer needed.

01:00:04.096 --> 01:00:07.576 align:middle
So the basic message here is,

01:00:08.036 --> 01:00:10.736 align:middle
U.S. economic performance
has been good.

01:00:11.526 --> 01:00:15.166 align:middle
The labor market has
strengthened substantially.

01:00:15.736 --> 01:00:18.486 align:middle
Every measure of
the labor market,

01:00:18.486 --> 01:00:21.556 align:middle
whether it's the narrow
unemployment rate,

01:00:21.836 --> 01:00:25.456 align:middle
the broader unemployment rate,
the number of people working

01:00:25.456 --> 01:00:29.016 align:middle
in part-time jobs who
want full-time work,

01:00:29.296 --> 01:00:33.546 align:middle
the level of job openings,
the quit rate, the difficulty

01:00:33.546 --> 01:00:37.886 align:middle
that firms are facing
in hiring workers,

01:00:38.146 --> 01:00:40.806 align:middle
the level of confidence
we see in surveys

01:00:41.116 --> 01:00:46.316 align:middle
about the labor market-all
of that is pointing to vast

01:00:46.316 --> 01:00:49.386 align:middle
and continuing improvement
in the labor market.

01:00:49.626 --> 01:00:53.336 align:middle
And we see sufficient
strength in the economy,

01:00:53.646 --> 01:00:58.936 align:middle
in terms of spending-that
growth, with its ups and downs,

01:00:59.226 --> 01:01:03.126 align:middle
but nevertheless is strong
enough-looks to be strong enough

01:01:03.126 --> 01:01:07.406 align:middle
in the medium term to
support ongoing improvement

01:01:07.406 --> 01:01:08.416 align:middle
in the labor market.

01:01:08.416 --> 01:01:10.116 align:middle
And all of that is good,

01:01:10.416 --> 01:01:14.586 align:middle
and I think that the American
people should feel the steps

01:01:14.586 --> 01:01:18.966 align:middle
we're taking to normalize
monetary policy are ones

01:01:18.966 --> 01:01:21.446 align:middle
that we feel are well justified,

01:01:21.816 --> 01:01:25.076 align:middle
given the very substantial
progress we've seen

01:01:25.786 --> 01:01:26.836 align:middle
in the economy.

01:01:27.526 --> 01:01:31.236 align:middle
Now, inflation is running
below where we want it to be,

01:01:31.236 --> 01:01:35.156 align:middle
and we've talked about that a
lot during this-the last hour.

01:01:36.836 --> 01:01:40.746 align:middle
This past year, where it's not
clear what the reasons are-I

01:01:41.166 --> 01:01:44.526 align:middle
think it's not been mysterious
in the past, but, one way

01:01:44.526 --> 01:01:47.466 align:middle
or another, we have
had four or five years

01:01:47.466 --> 01:01:51.266 align:middle
in which inflation is running
below our 2 percent objective,

01:01:51.676 --> 01:01:54.336 align:middle
and we are also committed
to achieving that.

01:01:54.886 --> 01:02:01.126 align:middle
So the monetary policy path
that we follow and the paths

01:02:01.236 --> 01:02:05.526 align:middle
that my colleagues are
writing down in our projections

01:02:05.526 --> 01:02:10.226 align:middle
as ones they think will be
appropriate given economic

01:02:10.226 --> 01:02:15.116 align:middle
conditions are ones that
we think are necessary

01:02:15.506 --> 01:02:18.386 align:middle
to move inflation
back up to 2 percent

01:02:18.766 --> 01:02:23.866 align:middle
and to maintain a strong labor
market on a sustainable basis.

01:02:24.336 --> 01:02:28.446 align:middle
And in making these judgments
about the path of policy,

01:02:28.756 --> 01:02:30.806 align:middle
we have to balance
various risks.

01:02:31.176 --> 01:02:37.486 align:middle
One risk is that if we
tighten policy too quickly,

01:02:37.746 --> 01:02:41.466 align:middle
we may find out that, although
we don't think this now,

01:02:41.826 --> 01:02:45.016 align:middle
that the inflation shortfall
is something that's going

01:02:45.016 --> 01:02:46.026 align:middle
to be persistent.

01:02:46.526 --> 01:02:48.436 align:middle
And if we tighten too quickly,

01:02:48.436 --> 01:02:52.686 align:middle
we could undermine
inflation performance-leave it

01:02:52.686 --> 01:02:56.756 align:middle
at too low a level- inflation
expectations could fall,

01:02:56.756 --> 01:02:59.936 align:middle
and that could become ingrained,
and that would be dangerous.

01:03:00.246 --> 01:03:02.836 align:middle
And so that's a reason
to be cautious

01:03:03.246 --> 01:03:06.756 align:middle
about raising interest rates

01:03:06.956 --> 01:03:09.136 align:middle
when inflation is
as low as it is.

01:03:09.376 --> 01:03:12.346 align:middle
But, on the other hand, we
have a strong labor market

01:03:12.346 --> 01:03:14.106 align:middle
and a low unemployment rate.

01:03:14.436 --> 01:03:17.826 align:middle
And although the pace of
job gains is not quite

01:03:17.826 --> 01:03:22.806 align:middle
as strong this year as, for
example, as it was in 2016,

01:03:23.296 --> 01:03:28.456 align:middle
we're still averaging 175,000
jobs a month this year,

01:03:28.836 --> 01:03:33.966 align:middle
which is quite a bit above the
pace of maybe 100 to 120,000

01:03:34.336 --> 01:03:38.946 align:middle
that would be consistent with
a stable unemployment rate

01:03:38.946 --> 01:03:41.966 align:middle
if labor force participation
begins to move

01:03:42.036 --> 01:03:43.986 align:middle
down in the manner we expect.

01:03:44.536 --> 01:03:50.046 align:middle
So if we don't do anything to
remove policy accommodation,

01:03:50.356 --> 01:03:55.316 align:middle
and the labor market tightens
and just continues to tighten,

01:03:55.546 --> 01:04:01.876 align:middle
as you mentioned, arguably-we
could, you know- arguably,

01:04:01.926 --> 01:04:06.406 align:middle
financial conditions overall
haven't tightened that much.

01:04:06.776 --> 01:04:08.736 align:middle
We think the economy
could overheat,

01:04:09.176 --> 01:04:12.686 align:middle
inflation could rise more
quickly and above our objective.

01:04:13.016 --> 01:04:15.236 align:middle
That's something that
would occur with a lag,

01:04:15.646 --> 01:04:20.116 align:middle
and that would force us later on
to tighten policy more rapidly

01:04:20.116 --> 01:04:21.386 align:middle
than would be ideal,

01:04:21.386 --> 01:04:24.286 align:middle
and we could risk a
recession if we did that.

01:04:24.286 --> 01:04:28.026 align:middle
So there are risks on both
sides to our objectives,

01:04:28.356 --> 01:04:31.386 align:middle
and most of my colleagues
and I have concluded

01:04:31.386 --> 01:04:37.526 align:middle
that a gradual path of rate
increases-while constantly

01:04:37.526 --> 01:04:42.416 align:middle
watching incoming data, being
open to revising our views

01:04:42.416 --> 01:04:46.836 align:middle
on the outlook, and
revising our expectations

01:04:46.836 --> 01:04:50.546 align:middle
about policy-is the best way
to manage that set of risks.

